READING=[passage("A harbour makes space for swimming", """
For decades, residents of the fictional port of Esten thought of the inner harbour as a place to work beside rather than enter. Cargo traffic had moved to a larger terminal, but the old quays remained behind fences. When a group proposed an outdoor swimming area, objections concerned more than water quality. Fishing crews still needed access, local businesses feared noise, and some residents doubted that a harbour could feel welcoming. The proposal's supporters began with temporary opening days so that people could experience the site before deciding whether a permanent facility belonged there.

Those early days used a floating enclosure close to a disused warehouse. Lifeguards supervised a small number of swimmers, and entry was free. The enclosure kept swimmers separate from boats but did not clean the water. Water testing was therefore essential, and the first opening was cancelled after heavy rainfall. Organisers displayed the cancellation reason at the gate rather than merely posting a notice online. This mattered because some visitors had travelled without checking the website. A disappointing first visit was less damaging to trust when the decision and its evidence were explained clearly.

Engineers later traced several drainage routes from streets above the quay. Rain could carry contaminated runoff into the harbour, creating conditions that differed from those measured on dry days. The city improved some drains and introduced a closure protocol based on tests and recent conditions. It did not promise that the swimming area would always be open. Planners considered this uncertainty in the design: a closed pool should not turn the entire waterfront into a dead end. The surrounding deck remained accessible for walking and sitting even when swimming was suspended.

Access to the water was another design problem. An ordinary ladder suited confident swimmers but not everyone who wanted to enter. The final enclosure included broad steps and a separate assisted-entry area. Changing cubicles were placed near the entrance, while lockers were visible from the staffed desk. The designers rejected a proposal to locate every service inside the old warehouse because it would force wet swimmers to cross a busy public route. The warehouse instead contained toilets and a classroom used for safety sessions, activities that did not require a direct path from the water.

The project also changed who controlled the quay at different times. Fishing crews retained an early-morning unloading period, after which removable barriers opened a wider pedestrian route. A permanent wall would have simplified separation but prevented flexibility. Harbour staff and facility managers met each week during the first summer to resolve problems with delivery times and equipment storage. This routine was less visible than the new deck, yet it made shared use possible. The design depended on agreements that could be adjusted when a fishing boat arrived later than expected or a public event required more space.

Counting visitors was straightforward; deciding whom the project served was not. An online survey produced many replies from confident swimmers who had visited repeatedly. Community worker Lena Moss also spoke to older residents at a nearby housing centre and parents at a school. Some liked the waterfront but did not want to swim. Others worried about the cost of lessons rather than admission. Their responses led to a small programme of free introductory sessions. The aim was not to persuade everyone to become a swimmer, but to remove an avoidable barrier for people who wanted to try.

By the third summer, the swimming facility had become one attraction among several. A café leased part of the warehouse and helped cover running costs, but its tables occupied only a marked section of the deck. The council wanted to avoid turning a publicly funded waterfront into a place where every seat required a purchase. Free benches remained along the edge, including some outside the busiest view. Maintenance budgets covered replacement of weathered boards and checks of underwater fittings. These recurring expenses were included in public reports alongside attendance, rather than hidden behind the celebration of construction.

Esten's fictional harbour was not transformed by a single architectural gesture. Its new use depended on testing, drainage work, access design, operating agreements and attention to people absent from the first survey. Nor did the change erase the port's working identity: fishing activity continued, and swimmers sometimes waited for a delivery to pass. The project suggests that opening a waterfront can mean negotiating a sequence of compatible uses rather than excluding everything that came before. Public access became credible because the city explained its limits and maintained the arrangements that allowed different activities to share a constrained space.
""",[
headings([
q("Paragraph A","v","temporary opening days"),
q("Paragraph B","ii","the decision and its evidence were explained clearly"),
q("Paragraph C","vii","a closed pool should not turn the entire waterfront into a dead end"),
q("Paragraph D","i","not everyone who wanted to enter"),
q("Paragraph E","iv","agreements that could be adjusted")],{'i':'Designing for different ways of entering','ii':'Explaining an unwelcome decision','iii':'Ending every commercial activity','iv':'The practical work of sharing space','v':'Testing public interest through experience','vi':'Cleaning the water with a floating wall','vii':'Keeping part of the site useful during closures'}),
tf([
q("The floating enclosure itself purified harbour water.","FALSE","did not clean the water"),
q("The deck could be used when swimming was prohibited.","TRUE","deck remained accessible for walking and sitting"),
q("The warehouse was demolished during construction.","FALSE","The warehouse instead contained toilets and a classroom"),
q("The café's rent covered the entire maintenance budget.","NOT GIVEN","helped cover running costs","A contribution is stated, not its proportion.")]),
completion([
q("The city developed a ____ protocol for unsafe conditions.","closure","introduced a closure protocol"),
q("Lockers could be seen from the staffed ____.","desk","visible from the staffed desk"),
q("Free introductory sessions responded partly to worries about the price of ____.","lessons","cost of lessons rather than admission"),
q("Reports included recurring maintenance expenses as well as ____.","attendance","alongside attendance")])]),
passage("Reading the ground without digging", """
An archaeological site does not end at the objects visible on its surface. Buried walls, filled ditches and changes in soil may extend across a much wider area. Excavation can reveal them in detail, but it is slow and changes the deposits it examines. Archaeologists therefore use survey techniques to decide where questions are most promising before opening trenches. In an imagined investigation at North Field, a team compared several ways of reading the ground. None offered an underground photograph. Each measured a physical property from which the team had to infer a possible history.

Survey specialist Ada Bell began with aerial images taken after a dry period. Some patches of vegetation had grown differently where buried features altered access to moisture. The marks were useful but inconsistent: a feature visible in one season could disappear in another. Bell compared images from several years and checked whether apparent lines followed modern farm machinery. A striking shape was not automatically ancient. She also recorded blank areas, resisting the temptation to draw a site boundary around only the most attractive marks. Absence in an image might reflect conditions rather than absence in the ground.

Geophysicist Ben Ortiz then used an instrument that detected small magnetic variations. In suitable conditions, disturbed soil and some heated materials can differ from surrounding deposits. At North Field, however, recent metal fragments produced strong signals that obscured subtler ones. Ortiz mapped these disturbances rather than simply smoothing them away. The cleaned display was easier to read, but he retained the raw measurements so that later users could see how processing affected interpretation. A visually tidy image can communicate certainty that the underlying observations do not deserve, especially when unexplained noise has been removed.

Another team, led by Celine Park, measured electrical resistance along selected lines. Moisture affects how easily current passes through soil, so a buried wall might differ from a filled ditch. Recent rain changed the overall readings, making comparisons between separate days difficult. Park introduced reference measurements in an unchanged strip of ground before each session. These did not solve every problem, but they helped identify variation associated with conditions rather than archaeology. Her results supported one of the magnetic features while leaving another uncertain. The disagreement was treated as a reason for a better question, not a competition to choose one winning instrument.

Local historian Dev Rao contributed maps and accounts of land use. A rectangular feature that excited the team as a possible early building appeared at the location of a much later livestock enclosure. The map did not prove the interpretation, because its scale and alignment were imperfect. It nevertheless offered an alternative that had to be considered. Rao also interviewed farmers who remembered changes in drainage. Their recollections were recorded with dates and qualifications rather than treated as either unquestionable truth or mere anecdote. Different evidence carried different uncertainties, and comparison required preserving those distinctions.

The team eventually opened two small trenches. One confirmed a stone foundation, while the other exposed a modern drain where a promising linear signal had appeared. This mixed result was more useful than a simple success story. It showed that the survey had located real differences in the ground without always identifying their cause correctly. The trench records were linked to the survey archive, including the unsuccessful interpretation. Future researchers would therefore be able to revisit the decision process instead of seeing only the feature that justified the team's original enthusiasm.

There was also a question of where not to dig. A patch containing fragile deposits was left untouched because the immediate research question could be answered elsewhere. Non-invasive survey did not make excavation unnecessary; it helped target an irreversible action. The landowner wanted assurance that the whole field would not be opened, and a public explanation described the planned limits. Protecting archaeological information involved preserving some uncertainty. A feature could remain an informed possibility until a future project had a compelling reason and a suitable method to investigate it more directly.

North Field's fictional investigation illustrates the difference between detecting a pattern and explaining it. Instruments extend perception, but their outputs become evidence through decisions about conditions, processing and comparison. A survey image can look authoritative while concealing several plausible interpretations. Good reporting makes that uncertainty usable rather than merely adding a vague warning at the end. The result is a map of questions as well as features: where evidence agrees, where it conflicts, and where a future observer might learn something new without repeating damage that has already been done.
""",[
match([
q("a record intended to show the effects of data processing","C","he retained the raw measurements"),
q("a reason for avoiding excavation in a particular place","G","immediate research question could be answered elsewhere"),
q("a modern historical explanation for an apparently older feature","E","a much later livestock enclosure"),
q("an example of excavation contradicting an initial interpretation","F","a modern drain where a promising linear signal had appeared")],dict.fromkeys('ABCDEFGH','Paragraph')),
match([
q("Compared images captured in different years","A","compared images from several years"),
q("Made reference measurements before each session","C","reference measurements in an unchanged strip"),
q("Recorded recollections about drainage changes","D","interviewed farmers who remembered changes in drainage"),
q("Identified interference caused by recent metal","B","recent metal fragments produced strong signals")],{'A':'Ada Bell','B':'Ben Ortiz','C':'Celine Park','D':'Dev Rao'},"Match each statement with the correct researcher."),
completion([
q("Vegetation marks partly reflected differences in access to ____.","moisture","altered access to moisture"),
q("Ortiz's instrument measured variations in the ground's ____ properties.","magnetic","small magnetic variations"),
q("Park measured electrical ____.","resistance","measured electrical resistance"),
q("The team linked trench records to the survey ____.","archive","linked to the survey archive"),
q("A good survey maps questions as well as ____.","features","questions as well as features")])]),
passage("Who should explain a decision?", """
When an institution uses a model to rank applications, explanation can appear to be an optional courtesy added after the result. A person receives a decision and perhaps a sentence describing the system's general purpose. Yet decisions allocate opportunities, and an explanation may be necessary for identifying an error or challenging an inappropriate rule. The question is not simply whether the model can produce a plausible account of its operation. It is whether the institution gives the affected person information that serves a practical purpose, together with a route for acting on it.

Different audiences require different explanations. An engineer investigating a calculation may need a detailed account of data transformations. An applicant may need to know that an outdated address caused the system to assign the wrong eligibility category. Providing the engineer's report to the applicant can satisfy a formal requirement while failing to help. Technical completeness and practical intelligibility are not identical. An institution should begin by asking what the recipient must be able to understand or do, rather than assuming that more mathematical detail always makes a decision more accountable.

Consider a fictional scholarship programme. Its ranking model awards additional weight to applicants from areas with limited educational resources. An applicant who recently moved is assigned to a better-resourced area using an old record. Explaining that neighbourhood data influenced the score is useful, but incomplete. The applicant needs to know which address was used and how to correct it. More importantly, the programme needs a procedure for reviewing the decision after correction. Information without a responsive process may merely describe an injustice accurately while leaving its consequence unchanged.

Explanation also has limits when the rule itself is disputed. A perfectly accurate account of a policy does not make the policy fair. People may agree on how a model treated distance from a training centre while disagreeing on whether distance should count at all. This disagreement cannot be resolved by showing that the software performed its calculation correctly. It requires institutional reasons and, where appropriate, public debate. Technical auditing remains valuable, but it answers a narrower question than the legitimacy of the objective that the institution has instructed the system to pursue.

Some critics argue that explanations will enable people to manipulate a system. The concern is real when a disclosed threshold allows someone to alter a superficial signal without meeting the policy's purpose. Nevertheless, secrecy can also shield errors and arbitrary rules. Institutions need to distinguish exploitable operational details from information necessary for a meaningful challenge. An applicant should not have to accept a demonstrably false record merely because the ranking method is commercially sensitive. The burden of finding an appropriate form of disclosure belongs primarily to the institution using the system, not to the person affected by it.

Nor is an appealing explanation necessarily faithful. A model can generate a simple story that sounds reasonable without identifying what actually determined its output. If staff repeat such stories without checking them, explanation becomes a technique for discouraging questions. A useful account should be tested against the decision process and should state where certainty is limited. In some cases the honest answer is that a particular contribution cannot be isolated reliably. That admission may be less satisfying than a confident narrative, but it gives a reviewer a more accurate basis for deciding whether further investigation is needed.

Human review is often offered as a remedy, although the phrase can conceal very different arrangements. A reviewer with no time, authority or additional information may simply confirm the original result. Meaningful review requires the ability to change it and a reasoned record of the judgement. It also requires attention to recurring patterns: correcting one applicant's address should lead the programme to ask whether similar errors affect others. Otherwise an appeal system can become a costly way of repairing individual cases while leaving a predictable source of harm operating unchanged.

Accountability therefore belongs to an institution, even when a complex model makes part of its work difficult to describe. A supplier may assist with analysis, but cannot absorb the public body's responsibility to justify the service it provides. Good explanations connect facts, rules and remedies in language suited to the recipient. Their value lies not in making every refusal pleasant or every dispute disappear, but in making mistakes contestable and policy choices visible. A decision becomes more accountable when the person receiving it can do more than admire the sophistication of the machinery behind it.
""",[
yn([
q("An explanation can be necessary for challenging an error.","YES","necessary for identifying an error or challenging an inappropriate rule"),
q("Greater mathematical detail always makes a decision more accountable.","NO","rather than assuming that more mathematical detail always makes a decision more accountable"),
q("The fictional scholarship programme received more than a thousand applications.","NOT GIVEN","Consider a fictional scholarship programme","No applicant total is given."),
q("Correct implementation proves that a policy objective is legitimate.","NO","a narrower question than the legitimacy of the objective"),
q("An honest account may need to acknowledge uncertainty.","YES","should state where certainty is limited")]),
mc([
q("What does the old-address example demonstrate?","D","Information without a responsive process","Explanation needs an effective means of correction.",['Eligibility should never depend on where a person lives.','Applicants are responsible for designing ranking systems.','Technical data should be kept separate from review staff.','Knowing the source of an error is insufficient without a remedy.']),
q("What distinction is made in paragraph D?","B","whether distance should count at all","Correct execution and justified policy are separate issues.",['Public debate is less reliable than every technical audit.','The correctness of a calculation differs from the fairness of its rule.','Distance cannot be measured accurately by software.','All applicants agree about the purpose of scholarship schemes.']),
q("How should institutions respond to manipulation concerns?","A","distinguish exploitable operational details from information necessary","Selective disclosure can support challenge without exposing every detail.",['Separate sensitive details from information required for challenge.','Withhold all reasons whenever a supplier requests secrecy.','Publish every operational threshold without considering effects.','Require applicants to explain the model before appealing.']),
q("What is the danger of a plausible but unfaithful explanation?","C","a technique for discouraging questions","An attractive story can hide the real basis of a decision.",['It forces reviewers to investigate too many accurate records.','It makes a faithful technical account impossible to produce.','It can discourage scrutiny without revealing the actual process.','It proves that the underlying decision must be incorrect.'])]),
completion([
q("A human reviewer needs the ____ to change a result.","authority","no time, authority or additional information"),
q("Review should identify recurring ____ rather than correcting only individual cases.","patterns","attention to recurring patterns"),
q("The institution retains ____ even when a supplier assists with analysis.","responsibility","the public body's responsibility"),
q("Explanations should connect facts, rules and ____.","remedies","connect facts, rules and remedies"),
q("The final goal is to make policy choices ____.","visible","policy choices visible")])])]
